Final Business Conclusion

The Amazon Brazil E-Commerce SQL Analysis project demonstrates how transactional data can support business decision-making across multiple functions.

The analysis highlights five major findings:

1. Regional concentration: São Paulo plays a central role in seller-side fulfillment activity and customer purchasing demand.

2. Revenue performance: Beauty & Health leads product revenue, while several other categories contribute substantial sales.

3. Payment behavior: Credit cards dominate recorded payment activity, making payment processing an important area for financial analysis.

4. Operational performance: The median delivery duration of 10 days provides a useful benchmark for evaluating logistics efficiency.

5. Customer engagement: The identification of 716 customers without recorded reviews provides a defined audience for post-purchase engagement initiatives.

Together, these findings demonstrate the value of SQL in transforming raw transactional records into business intelligence.

The project also identifies opportunities for deeper analysis, including seller performance scoring, customer segmentation, regional logistics optimization, product profitability, and demand forecasting.

Final Project Takeaway

This project demonstrates the application of MySQL to solve 14 real-world e-commerce business questions, interpret transactional data, identify operational patterns, and develop evidence-based recommendations for business improvement.
